                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_218\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix changes two return statements inside Input.parseRFC8601DateTime()'s\nisextendtime ('hh:mm:ss') and isextendfull ('hh:mm:ss.ssssss') branches from\n`return ret;` to `return ret * 1e-6;`, i.e. it converts the accumulated\nmicrosecond value (ret = hh*usPerHr + mm*usPerMin + ss*usPerSec [+ us]) into\nseconds before returning.\n\nBefore candidate_218, parseRFC8601DateTime() returned a `long` representing\nmicroseconds for ALL branches (the RFC8601 date/time branches via getUS(),\nand the bare-time \"extend\" branches inline), and every call site\n(Input.parseDoubles(), TimeSeriesDataInput, FileToArray) divided the result\nby 1e6 to obtain seconds. This was internally consistent.\n\nCandidate_218 (\"JS: refactor time parsing to work directly in simulation\ntime\") changed parseRFC8601DateTime()'s signature to return a `double`\nrepresenting simulation time *in seconds directly*, and removed the `/1e6`\ndivision from every call site (FileToArray.java, Input.parseDoubles(),\nTimeSeriesDataInput.java), because the is8601-date branches were rewritten\nto call simModel.calendarMillisToSimTime(...) which already returns seconds.\n\nHowever, this refactor did NOT update the isextendtime and isextendfull\nbranches (for plain 'hh:mm:ss' and 'hh:mm:ss.ssssss' duration strings, which\ndon't go through getUS/calendarMillisToSimTime). Those branches were left\nunchanged, still computing and returning `ret` as raw microseconds. Because\ncallers no longer divide by 1e6, any 'hh:mm:ss' or 'hh:mm:ss.ssssss' time\nvalue parsed through these two branches ends up 1,000,000x too large\n(returned as microseconds where seconds were expected).\n\nThis exact defect (isextendtime/isextendfull returning un-converted `ret`)\npersists unchanged through subsequent refactors (candidate_219, 220, etc.,\nwhich only touch the date-based branches) all the way up to the bug fix,\nwhich finally adds the missing `* 1e-6` conversion to these two branches.\nCandidate_218 is therefore the first commit that introduced the\ninconsistency/bug described in the fix commit message.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
